  <h2 id="why">Why Dental SEO Audits Matter</h2>
  <p>A dental website SEO audit reviews local visibility, treatment-page usefulness, practitioner evidence, patient privacy and technical search access. Record findings by URL and separate observed problems from proposed improvements; no audit can guarantee appointments or a position in Google's local results.</p>
  <p>Most dental websites share the same problems: generic template content written by the web design agency, no local optimisation beyond a basic Google listing, and service pages that describe procedures without addressing patient concerns.</p>

  <h2 id="local">Local SEO for Dentists</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Google Business Profile:</strong> verify eligibility, ownership, primary category, services, hours, location or service area and contact details. Google describes local ranking mainly through relevance, distance and prominence rather than one universal top factor.</li>
    <li><strong>Review evidence:</strong> compare current review volume, themes and recency with eligible nearby practices for a dated query sample. Google publishes no universal count or rating threshold; use a privacy-safe, platform-compliant request process that fits the jurisdiction and patient relationship.</li>
    <li><strong>Review responses:</strong> Respond helpfully at a cadence the practice can sustain, protecting patient confidentiality. Google recommends responding to reviews, but does not promise a ranking improvement or set a 48-hour threshold.</li>
    <li><strong>Business details:</strong> Keep the practice name, address and phone number accurate across the website, Business Profile and relevant directories. Normal formatting differences are less important than preventing materially wrong or duplicate information.</li>
    <li><strong>Relevant listings:</strong> verify profiles on platforms patients actually use and for which the practice is eligible. Correct material business details and duplicates, and measure referral value rather than requiring a US-specific directory list or promising authority from each citation.</li>
    <li><strong>Dental schema:</strong> Implement Dentist schema (a subtype of MedicalBusiness) with your specialties, accepted insurance, languages spoken, and opening hours.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="services">Service Page Optimisation</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Service-page scope:</strong> use a separate page when a treatment has a distinct patient task, verified offer, clinical owner and enough approved information to answer it. A practice does not need one URL for every treatment name.</li>
    <li><strong>Patient-focused content:</strong> Instead of clinical procedure descriptions, address what patients actually search for: "Does teeth whitening hurt?", "How long do dental implants last?", "How much do veneers cost in [city]?"</li>
    <li><strong>Before/after galleries:</strong> Visual proof of results is powerful for cosmetic dentistry pages. Include proper consent and context for all patient photos.</li>
    <li><strong>Location in titles:</strong> include a city or area only when the page represents a real location or substantiated service area and the modifier helps identify the page. Do not use city substitutions to imply offices or treatment availability that do not exist.</li>
    <li><strong>Call-to-action placement:</strong> Every service page should have a prominent "Book Appointment" button above the fold and a phone number visible on mobile.</li>
    <li><strong>Patient questions:</strong> answer the questions patients need for the specific treatment and route medical claims to a qualified reviewer. FAQ markup must match visible content and does not guarantee a rich result.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="trust">Patient Trust Signals</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Team photos and bios:</strong> Patients want to see who will be treating them. Include professional photos and detailed bios for every dentist, hygienist, and team member.</li>
    <li><strong>Credentials display:</strong> List qualifications, certifications, professional memberships (ADA, BDA, specialist boards), and years of experience prominently.</li>
    <li><strong>Patient testimonials:</strong> Display reviews on your website (with permission). Video testimonials are particularly effective for dental practices.</li>
    <li><strong>Office photos and virtual tours:</strong> High-quality photos of your waiting room, treatment rooms, and equipment reduce patient anxiety about visiting.</li>
    <li><strong>Insurance and pricing transparency:</strong> state accepted plans and explain how patients can obtain an accurate estimate where local rules and the practice's billing model allow it. Treat this as patient information rather than a claimed ranking factor.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="technical">Technical Quick Wins</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mobile click-to-call:</strong> Your phone number must be a clickable tel: link on mobile. Test it — many dental websites display phone numbers as images or non-clickable text.</li>
    <li><strong>Page speed:</strong> Dental websites are often built on heavy WordPress themes with large image sliders. Target LCP under 2.5 seconds. Optimise images and remove unnecessary plugins.</li>
    <li><strong>Online booking integration:</strong> If online scheduling is offered, test load time, completion, errors, privacy and accessibility on representative devices. Use funnel evidence to quantify abandonment instead of assigning a loss to a five-second threshold.</li>
    <li><strong>SSL certificate:</strong> Required for patient trust and SEO. If your site still has HTTP pages, fix immediately.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="content">Content Opportunities</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Condition pages:</strong> create a condition page only when it answers a distinct patient need with clinically approved, current information and a truthful next step.</li>
    <li><strong>Insurance information:</strong> explain plans the practice actually accepts and how patients can verify coverage. Use separate insurer pages only when each supports a distinct, maintainable task.</li>
    <li><strong>Area pages:</strong> create a neighbourhood or suburb page only when the practice genuinely serves it and can provide sourceable details that differ from the main location page. Repeated city-name substitutions do not create local value.</li>
    <li><strong>Emergency dentistry:</strong> "Emergency dentist near me" is high-intent, high-value. Create a dedicated emergency page with clear instructions and after-hours contact details.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="checklist">Dental SEO Checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Business Profile verified and accurate</strong> with current services, photos and hours</li>
    <li><strong>Dated review comparison</strong> with no invented count or rating threshold</li>
    <li><strong>Review response ownership</strong> with a privacy-safe process</li>
    <li><strong>NAP consistent</strong> across all directories</li>
    <li><strong>Separate service pages</strong> only for verified, distinct treatment tasks</li>
    <li><strong>Accurate location wording</strong> supported by real operations</li>
    <li><strong>FAQ schema</strong> on service pages</li>
    <li><strong>Dentist schema</strong> with specialties and insurance</li>
    <li><strong>Team bios with photos</strong> and credentials</li>
    <li><strong>Click-to-call working</strong> on mobile</li>
    <li><strong>Online booking fast</strong> and mobile-friendly</li>
    <li><strong>Emergency dentistry page</strong> with after-hours info</li>
  </ul>
</ContentLayout>
